# shir_nodes/split.node — primitive: split a string into an array by a delimiter.
# Every language has split(). Composition base for wc -l / wc -w / cut / head / tail.

node Split
tag "Split"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field delim: string
field is_regex: bool
# in_place: the source buffer may be DESTROYED (delimiters stamped with
# NUL) — set by liveness analysis when the source var is dead after the
# split. C renders an in-place tokenizer on the var's own storage; GC'd
# string languages ignore it (immutable strings — flag meaningless).
field in_place: optional_bool
spec: TextOps#split_ws_len
spec: TextOps#split_ws_fields_clean
spec: TextOps#intercalate_split_on
spec: TextOps#cut_keeps_trailing_empty_field
# The field LIST is the meaning. What is machine-checked is its count
# (split_ws_len: dropping the empty fields is what makes the count equal the
# ws -w count), its shape (split_ws_fields_clean: no empty field, no
# whitespace inside one), and now its BYTES: `intercalate_split_on` says
# joining the fields back with the delimiter recovers the input exactly, so no
# byte is dropped, duplicated or reordered by the split — the per-field byte
# identity this note used to list as an open target. `cut_keeps_trailing_empty_field`
# pins the one case a lowering gets wrong: a trailing delimiter leaves a
# trailing EMPTY field (unlike a whitespace split), which is why `cut -f2` on
# "a," prints the empty line. Which FIELDS a later node takes is
# FieldExtract's obligation (TextOps#select_subset and friends); a regex
# delimiter is outside this model.
